## 2025-08-16 15:01:45 Drift-Corrected Clock Generator Implementation
# ISSUE: Clock generator timing drift causing 1.8 BPM error (120 BPM → 118.1 BPM detection)
# ROOT CAUSE: Cumulative timing errors in relative tick scheduling algorithm
# PROBLEM ANALYSIS:
#   - Expected: 2.000s per measure (4 beats at 120 BPM)
#   - Actual: 2.030-2.057s per measure (consistently 1.5% slow)
#   - Cause: thread::sleep() overhead + tick scheduling drift + processing delays
#
# DRIFT-CORRECTED ALGORITHM IMPLEMENTED:
#   1. Absolute timing: Calculate target time for each tick from start_time
#   2. Removed relative timing: No more duration_since(last_tick) comparisons
#   3. Drift compensation: Compare actual time vs expected tick schedule
#   4. Adaptive sleep: Sleep until just before next tick time for precision
#
# NEW TIMING LOGIC:
#   - target_time = start_time + (tick_count * tick_interval)
#   - Send tick when now >= target_time
#   - Adaptive sleep: sleep for 80% of remaining time, wake early to avoid overshoot
#   - Behind schedule: minimal microsleep to yield CPU
#
# ENHANCED DIAGNOSTICS:
#   - Shows expected vs actual measure duration
#   - Displays timing error per measure
#   - Reports drift correction effectiveness
#   - Format: "timing: expected 2.000s, actual 2.001s, error 0.001s, drift 0.000s"
#
# EXPECTED RESULT: Clock generator should now maintain precise 120.0 BPM
# SEQUENCER DETECTION: Should now see 120.0 BPM instead of 118.1 BPM
# STATUS: Drift-corrected clock generator ready for testing - should eliminate tempo discrepancy

